🎭 The One-Sentence Difference (memorize this first)

Overloading = SAME class, SAME method name, DIFFERENT parameters, decided at COMPILE time 🛠️
Overriding = PARENT-CHILD classes, SAME method name, SAME parameters, decided at RUNTIME 🏃

If that doesn't click yet, don't worry — let's break it down with analogies because that one-liner alone confuses people 😂

1️⃣ Method Overloading — "Same Name, Different Costume" 👗

The analogy: Imagine you have ONE friend named "Order" 🍕 who takes food orders. You can call him in different ways:

Order(pizza) → he brings you pizza
Order(pizza, coke) → he brings you pizza + coke
Order(pizza, coke, fries) → he brings you all three

Same person "Order", but WHAT he does depends on HOW MANY things you hand him (or what TYPE of things). That's overloading — same method name, but different parameter lists (different number of params, or different types of params).

Key rule: It happens in the SAME class. No inheritance needed at all.

java
class Calculator {
    // version 1: two integers
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    // version 2: three integers — different parameter COUNT
    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    // version 3: two doubles — different parameter TYPE
    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

// Usage
Calculator calc = new Calculator();
calc.add(2, 3);         // ✅ calls version 1 → returns 5
calc.add(2, 3, 4);      // ✅ calls version 2 → returns 9
calc.add(2.5, 3.5);     // ✅ calls version 3 → returns 6.0

Java looks at what you PASSED IN and picks the right version — and it does this at compile time 🛠️ (before the program even runs, Java already knows which add() you meant based on the arguments). That's why it's also called "compile-time polymorphism" or "static polymorphism."

⚠️ Important gotcha interviewers test: You CANNOT overload just by changing the return type alone. This is WRONG ❌:

java
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
double add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } // ❌ ERROR — same params, Java can't tell these apart!

The parameters MUST differ (count, type, or order) — return type alone doesn't count. This is a classic trick question 🪤

2️⃣ Method Overriding — "Same Name, New Personality" 🎪

The analogy: Remember our Animal → Dog/Cat example from before? Think of it like this: your DAD has a signature move — "how he greets people" 👋 (a firm handshake). YOU inherited that same "greet people" method from him, but YOU do it your own way — maybe you fist-bump instead 👊. Same method NAME (greet), but you gave it your OWN version.

Key rule: This ONLY happens with inheritance — a child class provides its own implementation of a method that already exists in the parent class. The method signature (name + parameters) must be EXACTLY the same.

java
class Animal {
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Some generic animal sound...");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void makeSound() {   // SAME name, SAME parameters (none), but new behavior
        System.out.println("Woof Woof! 🐶");
    }
}

// Usage — THE INTERESTING PART
Animal myPet = new Dog();  // reference type is Animal, actual object is Dog
myPet.makeSound();  // prints "Woof Woof!" — decided at RUNTIME, not compile time!

Here's the magic 🪄 — even though myPet is DECLARED as type Animal, Java looks at what the object ACTUALLY IS at runtime (a Dog) and calls the Dog's version. This decision happens while the program is RUNNING, not before — that's why it's called "runtime polymorphism" or "dynamic polymorphism."

Rules that interviewers LOVE to test:

Method name + parameters must be IDENTICAL to the parent's method (unlike overloading, where params must differ)
The @Override annotation isn't mandatory but it's good practice — Java will warn you if you're not actually overriding anything (like if you typo the method name)
You need inheritance — no parent-child relationship = no overriding possible